course: chinese-street
lesson: 13
title: In the playground: come on, your turn!
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The playground is where you learn fastest, because everyone is shouting. «kuài lái!» (come here, quick!), «kuài diǎnr!» (hurry up!), «zǒu ba!» (let's go!) — kuài means fast, and you hear it all day.
explain: Taking turns has its own words: gāi nǐ le (your turn), gāi wǒ le (my turn), and from the teacher pái duì! (line up!). Shout «gāi wǒ le» while grabbing and a quarrel starts; say it with your hand up and it's fair.
explain: In tag the catcher yells «zhuā zhù le!» (got you!), on sports day every Chinese parent roars «jiāyóu!» (come on! literally «add oil»), and from the edge of the game a shy voice says «wǒ yě yào wánr» (I want to play too).
explain: And «děng yíxià!» (wait a moment!) means three seconds from a friend and about an hour from a parent.
fact: kuài lái («KWIGH LIGH?») means come here, quick; the answer is lái le! (coming!).
fact: kuài diǎnr («KWIGH DYAR») means hurry up; normal between friends, but rude if snapped at a grown-up.
fact: zǒu ba («DZOE ba») means let's go; zǒu alone, shouted, can also mean go away!
fact: yìqǐ wánr ba («ee-CHEE WAR ba») means let's play together; the r curl in wánr is northern, southern kids say wán.
fact: gāi nǐ le («GUY nee luh») means your turn; gāi means should, so the same words can also mean «serves you right!», and the tone of voice tells you which.
fact: gāi wǒ le («GUY waw luh») means my turn; grabbing while you say it starts a quarrel.
fact: děng yíxià («DUNG ee-SHYAH») means wait a moment.
fact: kuài pǎo («KWIGH POW») means run!; pǎo (run) dips, while falling pào is a bubble or a cannon.
fact: zhuā zhù le («JWAH JOO luh») means got you!, shouted in tag (zhuō rén); the reply might be méi zhuā zhù! (you didn't!).
fact: jiāyóu («JYAH-yo?», literally «add oil») means come on, go for it; it has nothing to do with real oil any more.
fact: pái duì («PIGH? DWAY») means queue up; pushing in is chā duì, and calling out «bié chā duì!» (no pushing in!) is fair.
fact: wǒ yě yào wánr («waw YEH YOW WAR») means I want to play too; yě means too, and the welcome answer is lái ba! (come on then).
exercise: Ming waves from the slide: «kuài lái!». What does he want? | You to come here, quick | You to go away and leave him | You to be quiet | You to wait at the bottom
exercise: What does kuài diǎnr mean? | Hurry up! | Slow down! | A little bit | Well done!
exercise: To whom is «kuài diǎnr!» snapped rude? | A grown-up | A friend | Your little brother | Your dog
exercise: zǒu ba means | Let's go! | Let's eat! | Let's stop! | Let's hide!
exercise: What can a shouted «zǒu!» on its own also mean? | Go away! | Thank you! | Run faster! | Good night!
exercise: How do you invite a friend to play together? | yìqǐ wánr ba | gāi nǐ le | děng yíxià | pái duì
exercise: What does gāi nǐ le mean on the swings? | Your turn | My turn | Wait for me | Got you
exercise: What else can gāi nǐ le mean, in a different voice? | Serves you right! | Well done, mate! | Go home now! | You're first!
exercise: How do you claim your go fairly? | Say gāi wǒ le with your hand up | Shout gāi wǒ le and grab the swing | Push your way to the front | Say nothing and take it
exercise: děng yíxià means | Wait a moment | Hurry up | Come back | Good luck
exercise: What does kuài pǎo mean? | Run! | Bubble! | Stop! | Hide!
exercise: In tag, the catcher touches you and shouts | zhuā zhù le! | jiāyóu! | pái duì! | zǒu ba!
exercise: What does jiāyóu literally mean? | Add oil | Run fast | Good luck | Well done
exercise: When do Chinese parents shout jiāyóu? | Cheering kids on, like on sports day | When the car needs petrol at the station | When kids are being naughty | At bedtime, to say sleep well
exercise: The teacher says pái duì. What do you do? | Line up | Sit down | Go home | Run
exercise: What is chā duì? | Pushing in | Lining up | Leaving the line | A dance
exercise: How do you ask to join a game? | wǒ yě yào wánr | bù wánr le | gāi nǐ le | zǒu ba
exercise: Where does the r in wánr come from? | Northern speech; southern kids say wán | It means «very», so it's «play a lot», «play loads» | It is a spelling mistake in books | It shows a question
test: «ming yelled jiāyóu at me on sports day, I don't need petrol??» What did he mean? | Come on, go for it! | Get some oil, mate | You're far too slow! | Go home
test: You boasted you'd win the race, then tripped. Ming laughs «gāi nǐ le!». What does he mean? | Serves you right! | It's your turn now | Well done, nice try | Wait for me
test: «his mum said děng yíxià an hour ago» What's going on? | A parent's «wait a moment» can be long | She really did say «wait an hour», word for word | She meant hurry up, get ready | She meant no
test: A kid pushes in at the tuck shop. What can you fairly say? | bié chā duì! | jiāyóu! | kuài lái! | gāi wǒ le!
test: You snap «kuài diǎnr!» at Ming's dad while he finds his keys. How does it sound? | Rude to a grown-up | Very polite and kind | Like «good luck» | Like «thank you, sir»
test: What would happen if you shouted «kuài pào!» with a falling tone? | You'd shout «quick, cannon!» instead of «run» | Everyone would run, because it's just the same word | It's the polite way to say «run» | It means «got you»
test: «zhuā zhù le!» «méi zhuā zhù!» What's the argument? | Got you! — No you didn't! | Run! — Wait! | My turn! — Your turn! | Hello! — Goodbye!
test: Ming says «wán» not «wánr». Is he wrong? | No, southern speakers say wán | Yes, wán on its own means no, so it's wrong | Yes, he forgot half the word | No, wán means sleep
test: A lonely kid stands at the edge and says «wǒ yě yào wánr». What's a kind answer? | lái ba! | zǒu! | bù xíng! | děng yíxià, for ever
test: In a cartoon a girl grabs the ball yelling «gāi wǒ le!» and the game is ruined. What would be fair? | Hand up, «gāi wǒ le», and wait | Grab harder next time | Run away with the ball | Shout zǒu! at the others
test: «shut up and zǒu ba, the park's closing» What does he want? | Let's go | Go away for ever | Stay here | Be quiet and sit
exam: Identify the expression customarily employed to indicate that it is the hearer's turn. | gāi nǐ le | gāi wǒ le | děng yíxià | zǒu ba
exam: Ascertain the literal signification of the exhortation jiāyóu. | To add oil | To run swiftly | To wait patiently | To form a queue
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the expression pái duì. | It instructs one to form a queue; pushing in is termed chā duì | It is an instruction to disperse and leave the queue | It is a cheer employed at sporting events | It denotes victory in a game
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expression wǒ yě yào wánr? | It is a request to be permitted to join a game | It is a refusal to continue playing the game any longer | It is an accusation of cheating | It is an instruction to hurry
